  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMost of the Flood Warnings have been allowed to expire. There \nis one last Flood Warning in effect for residual flooding/road \nclosures in SE Albany, SW Rensselaer, NE Greene and NW/central \nColumbia counties through 630 PM. Water levels will continue to \ngradually recede. No additional river flooding is anticipated. \nScattered showers this afternoon into this evening will be \ngenerally light and will not lead to any additional flooding. \nThere may be a few brief/isolated downpours, but not widespread \nenough to cause any hydro concerns. The showers should gradually\ndissipate tonight as the storm system finally pulls farther \naway. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOther than an isolated/brief shower or T-storm north/east of \nAlbany on Friday, dry conditions are expected to persist through\nSunday morning as high pressure moves across the area. The next\nchance for more widespread showers/T-storms arrives Sunday \nafternoon into Monday as a cold front and upper level trough \nmove east across our area, associated with a cyclone tracking NE\nacross the St. Lawrence Valley. Most of the global/AI guidance \nhave more of a progressive northern stream system, as the upper \ntrough splits with the southern piece tracking well south of our\narea. This is the most likely scenario at this time. While the \nsystem is expected to be progressive, PWAT anomalies increase \nto +1 to +2 STDEV along with at least some modest instability. \nSo convective elements will likely be embedded in any areas of \nshowers Sun P.M. into Mon. Areas where there are any persistent\nor repeated downpours could see renewed flooding, especially in\nlocations that received heavy rainfall from this past storm. \nNBM probabilities for > 1.00\" of rain are around 30-60% \nnorth/west of Albany, however embedded convection could result \nin 1-2+\" in some spots. Also, soils will still be mostly \nsaturated from the extreme rainfall this week so it won't take \nmuch to result in flash flooding in some areas. Timing has \nshifted slower to Sunday night, which could limit convection, so\nWPC has scaled back to a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 4) for\neastern New York with the afternoon update. Will continue to \nmonitor trends.",
